"Conjugate Duality and Optimization | SIAM Publications Library (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/book/10.1137/1.9781611970524)\nciteturn429search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 52.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; Conjugate Duality and Optimization ... Tyrrell Rockafellar ... However, in order to get to the fundamentals, it is convenient for us to begin by considering only the following abstract optimization problem: minimize ƒ(x) for x ∈ C, where C is a subset of a real linear space X, and ƒ:C → [−∞, +∞].\n\n  * Description\n\nProvides a relatively brief introduction to conjugate duality in both finite- and infinite-dimensional problems. An emphasis is placed on the fundamental importance of the concepts of Lagrangian function, saddle-point, and saddle-value. General examples are drawn from nonlinear programming, approximation, stochastic programming, the calculus of variations, and optimal control.\n\n  * ### Keywords\n\n    * conjugate duality,\n    * Lagrangian function,\n    * saddle-point,\n    * saddle-value\n\n\n### Front Matter\n\npp. i–x\n\n  * Excerpt\n  * PDF\n\nExcerpt\n\nThe front matter includes the title page, series page, copyright page, TOC, and preface.\n\n[Input]\n\n### 1. Conjugate Duality and Optimization\n\n  * R. Tyrrell Rockafellar\n\npp. 1–74\n\n  * Excerpt\n  * PDF\n\nExcerpt\n\n1. The role of convexity and duality. In most situations involving optimization there is a great deal of mathematical structure to work with. In particular, if ƒ is a real-valued function on a set C ⊂ X and we define ƒ (x) =+∞ for x ∉ C, then ƒ is convex as an extended-real-valued function on X if and only if (i) C is convex, and (ii) ƒ is convex relative to C in the classical sense that the inequality ƒ ( (1−λ) x1 +λ x2 ) ≦ (1−λ) ƒ ( x1 ) +λƒ ( x2 ) if0<λ<1 1.1 always holds. A convex function is proper if it is the extension in this way of a real-valued function on a nonempty set C, i.e., if dom ƒ≠∅ and ƒ (x) >−∞ for all x ∈ X.\n\nConvex optimization problems, that is, problems which can be expressed as above with ƒ convex, have many pleasant properties:\n\n(a) Local versus global analysis. One does not have to contend computationally or theoretically with isolated local minima or “stationary points” which do not solve the overall problem.\n\n\nE-book (Online Access, No PDF download)\n\nMEMBER$31.82\n\nLog In\n\nMembers: log in to access member price\n\nNONMEMBER$45.45\n\nAdd to cart\n\nSoftcover\n\nMEMBER$35.35\n\nLog In\n\nMembers: log in to access member price\n\nNONMEMBER$50.50\n\nAdd to cart\n\n  * Published:1974\n\nISBN:978-0-89871-013-7\n\neISBN:978-1-61197-052-4\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611970524\n\nBook Series Name:CBMS-NSF Regional Conference Series in Applied Mathematics\n\nBook Code:CB16\n\nBook Pages:x + 74--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjugate Duality and Optimization | 1. Conjugate Duality and Optimization (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/abs/10.1137/1.9781611970524.ch1)\nciteturn429search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.6 years ago; Crawled: 2.4 years ago; Conjugate Duality and Optimization ... Conjugate Duality and Optimization ... Tyrrell Rockafellar and R.\n\nCBMS-NSF Regional Conference Series in Applied Mathematics\n\nConjugate Duality and Optimization\n\n# 1. Conjugate Duality and Optimization\n\nAuthors: R. Tyrrell Rockafellar and R. Tyrrell RockafellarAuthors Info & Affiliations\n\nPages 1 - 74\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611970524.ch1\n\n## Abstract\n\n1. The role of convexity and duality. In most situations involving optimization there is a great deal of mathematical structure to work with. However, in order to get to the fundamentals, it is convenient for us to begin by considering only the following abstract optimization problem: minimize ƒ(x) for x ∈ C, where C is a subset of a real linear space X, and ƒ:C → [−∞, +∞].\n\nIf we define (or redefine) ƒ so that ƒ\\left(x\\right)=+\\infty for x ∉ C then minimizing ƒ over C is equivalent to minimizing the new ƒ over all of X. Thus no generality is lost in our abstract model if we restrict attention to the case where C=X . This conceptual and notational simplification is one of the main reasons for admitting extended-real-valued functions to the theory of optimization. \n1.1\n\nalways holds. A convex function is proper if it is the extension in this way of a real-valued function on a nonempty set C, i.e., if dom ƒ\\ne \\varnothing and ƒ\\left(x\\right)>-\\infty for all x ∈ X.\n\nConvex optimization problems, that is, problems which can be expressed as above with ƒ convex, have many pleasant properties:\n\n(a) Local versus global analysis. One does not have to contend computationally or theoretically with isolated local minima or “stationary points” which do not solve the overall problem.\n\n## Information & Authors\n\n### Information\n\n#### Published In\n\nImage: cover image CBMS-NSF Regional Conference Series in Applied Mathematics\n\nConjugate Duality and Optimization\n\nPages: 1 - 74\n\nDOI: 10.1137/1.9781611970524.ch1\n\nISBN (International Standard Book Number) (Print): 978-0-89871-013-7\n\nISBN (International Standard Book Number) (Online): 978-1-61197-052-4\n\n#### Copyright\n\nAll rights reserved. Printed in the United States of America. No part of this book may be reproduced, stored, or transmitted in any manner without the written permission of the publisher. For information, write to the Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics, 3600 Market Street, Philadelphia, PA 19104-2688.\n\n#### History\n\nPublished online: 28 March 2012\n\n#### Permissions\n\nRequest permissions for this article.\n\nRequest Permissions\n\n#### Keywords\n\n  1. conjugate duality\n  2. Lagrangian function\n  3. saddle-point\n  4. saddle-value\n\n#### Keywords\n\n  1. 12\n\n### Authors\n\n#### Affiliations\n\nR. Tyrrell Rockafellar\n\nUniversity of Washington, Seattle, Washington\n\nView all articles by R. Tyrrell Rockafellar\n\nR. Tyrrell Rockafellar\n-------------------